  <div class="prose">
    <h2>A worked example: the default $400,000 home</h2>
    <p>With 20% down, the loan is $320,000. At 6.5% over 30 years, principal and interest come to $2,022.62 a month. Adding the default property tax ($4,800 a year, or $400 a month) and insurance ($1,400 a year, or $116.67 a month) brings the housing payment to $2,539.28. No PMI applies because the down payment is 20%.</p>
    <p>Over 30 years that loan costs $408,142 in interest, more than the amount borrowed. Most of it is paid early: the 1st payment sends $1,733.33 to interest and just $289.28 to principal.</p>

    <h2>How much the rate matters</h2>
    <p>Monthly principal and interest on the same $320,000, 30-year loan:</p>
