  <div class="prose">
    <p>Each half point moves the payment by roughly $100 a month on this loan, or $37,000 to $38,000 over 30 years. That is why it pays to get quotes from several lenders on the same day and compare the Loan Estimates line by line.</p>

    <h2>15 years vs. 30 years, and extra payments</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A 15-year term</strong> at the same 6.5% raises the payment to $2,787.54 but cuts total interest to $181,758, saving about $226,000. In practice 15-year loans usually carry lower rates, which widens the gap.</li>
      <li><strong>An extra $200 a month</strong> on the 30-year loan pays it off in about 23 years and 5 months and saves $105,429 in interest, while keeping the lower required payment as a fallback if money gets tight.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>PMI: what it costs and when it ends</h2>
    <p>With less than 20% down on a conventional loan, lenders usually require private mortgage insurance. This calculator assumes 0.5% of the loan per year if you leave the field blank; actual rates depend on credit score and down payment. On a $360,000 loan with 10% down, 0.5% is $150 a month.</p>
    <p>Under the federal Homeowners Protection Act, you can ask to cancel PMI on a conventional loan once the balance reaches 80% of the home's original value, and it must end automatically at 78% if you are current on payments. FHA loans work differently: their mortgage insurance premium generally lasts for the life of the loan when the down payment is under 10%.</p>

    <h2>Checking affordability</h2>
    <p>A long-standing lender guideline keeps the total housing payment at or below about 28% of gross monthly income. The $2,539.28 payment in the example works out to an income of about $108,800 a year. Lenders also look at total debt payments, and approval amounts are often higher than what is comfortable. Leave room for maintenance, often budgeted at 1% or more of the home's value a year, plus utilities and repairs the payment does not cover.</p>
  </div>
